<TopicPage slug="subjects/urban-planning/foundational/planning-history-and-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Planning is the deliberate attempt to shape how cities grow — and its history is a
    century-long argument about <em>who</em> gets to do the shaping, and for whom. The
    field's founding heroes drew total visions: Haussmann's boulevards carved through
    medieval Paris, Howard's garden cities sketched on a diagram, Burnham's monumental
    plan for Chicago ("make no little plans"). The mid-twentieth century bureaucratised
    that visionary impulse into the <em>rational model</em> — define goals, gather data,
    generate alternatives, evaluate, choose — enforced day to day through zoning. Then
    came the backlash, from three directions at once: Jane Jacobs showed that master
    plans were strangling exactly the street-level vitality they claimed to serve;
    Charles Lindblom showed that real decisions are incremental "muddling through," not
    synoptic surveys; and Sherry Arnstein showed that "participation" was often theatre.
    A later generation rebuilt the field around communication and justice instead of
    blueprints.
  </p>
  <p>
    Understanding this argument is not academic decoration, because every zoning hearing
    is a live collision between all three positions: the rational model speaks (the
    traffic study), communicative practice performs (the public consultation), and the
    just city asks the question that ends speeches (who benefits, and who is displaced?).
    A planner without all three vocabularies is fluent in only part of their own job.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chapter follows the field's arc in order: how cities and planning co-evolved from
    sanitary reform to the modernist orthodoxies; the rise, critique and demotion of
    rational planning theory; the communicative turn; the just-city challenge; and the
    ethics that binds the whole tradition into a daily practice.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three theories, in one ascent: rational → communicative → just" widget="equationStep" config={ptCfg} />

  <h2>The evolution of cities and planning</h2>
  <p>
    Modern planning crystallised between 1890 and 1930, but its seed was sanitation. The
    industrial city of the 1840s was a mortality machine — cholera epidemics traced to
    contaminated wells, slums whose death rates scandalised statistics — and the first
    urban intervention that mattered was engineering: sewers, piped water, paved streets.
    Haussmann's Paris (1853–1870) remains the grandest statement of the top-down model:
    boulevards, aqueducts, sewers and parks imposed by an emperor's decree, displacing
    thousands — magnificent, and a standing argument about whom such magnificence
    served. The sanitary insight (that urban form determines urban health) became the
    profession's charter, and it is why planning still shares intellectual DNA with
    public health.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three overlapping movements then built the profession. <strong>The City
    Beautiful</strong> movement (its showcase the 1893 Chicago World's Fair) treated
    monumental design as civic reform: if the city looked noble, citizens would be ennobled
    — a theory civic centres and beaux-arts boulevards made visible across America.
    Ebenezer Howard's <strong>garden cities</strong> (1898) proposed the opposite scale:
    small self-contained towns of about 30,000, ringed by permanent greenbelt, balancing
    town and country — built at Letchworth (1903) and Welwyn, and ancestor to the entire
    new-towns movement from the UK's postwar programme to planned capitals.
    <strong>Zoning</strong> gave planning its everyday legal machinery: New York's 1916
    resolution (prompted in part by the Equitable Building's tower walling out its
    neighbours' light) separated uses and shaped building bulk, and the US Supreme
    Court constitutionalised the device in <em>Euclid v. Ambler</em> (1926). The
    modernist <strong>Athens Charter</strong> (CIAM, 1933) then consolidated a
    functionalist orthodoxy — separate the city into dwelling, work, recreation and
    circulation — which dominated postwar rebuilding on both sides of the Atlantic and
    produced the towers-in-parks estates, urban-renewal clearances and highway cuts that
    a generation of critics, led from the sidewalk by Jacobs, dismantled.
  </p>
  <p>
    The postwar decades tested the orthodoxy at full scale, and the results are the
    field's shared case studies. Brasília (inaugurated 1960) built a modernist capital
    entire — monumental axes, superquadras, functions strictly separated — visually
    stunning and, its critics note, a city hard to walk and hostile to street life, an
    argument about design for living that continues. Britain's New Towns Act (1946)
    delivered Howard's scale-up: dozens of planned towns absorbing postwar population,
    largely judged a quiet success even as their inner-city "comprehensive development"
    cousins cleared whole neighbourhoods in the name of blight. American urban renewal
    became the definitive cautionary tale: federally funded clearance of "slums" that
    were often functioning (if poor) communities, replaced by housing projects and
    highways whose disruption of social networks outlasted any building. The civil-rights-era
    protest summed it up in a phrase the literature still quotes: what was called urban
    renewal, James Baldwin said in 1963, too often meant what Black communities
    experienced as removal. Planning's post-1970s turn toward preservation, community
    development corporations and consultation is the direct institutional scar tissue.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="key moments in planning thought, 1853–2010" widget="timeline" config={upTl} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> zoning is a neutral safety regulation, a technical
    map of what belongs where. Historically it has been at least as much a sorting
    machine: minimum lot sizes and single-family-only districts — legal in the US for a
    century — function to exclude cheaper housing, and were often designed to, in living
    memory alongside explicitly discriminatory tools like redlining (the 1930s federal
    maps that graded minority neighbourhoods as unbankable, whose effects persist in
    wealth and tree cover to this day). The profession's current debates — upzoning,
    missing-middle housing, exclusionary suburbia — are the direct descendants of this
    history, which is why the just-city theorists insist that planning instruments are
    never value-neutral.
  </p>

  <h2>Rational, communicative and just-city theories</h2>
  <p>
    The mid-century textbook answer to "how should planners decide?" was the
    <em>rational-comprehensive model</em>: define goals, gather data, generate all
    feasible alternatives, evaluate each against criteria, choose the optimum, implement.
    For some decisions this genuinely is the right tool — a new water treatment plant has
    a knowable goal, measurable alternatives and a public interest that no hearing can
    dissolve — and cost-benefit analysis in this tradition remains infrastructure
    planning's workhorse. But as a general theory it had two fatal blind spots, exposed
    in two famous critiques. Charles Lindblom (1959) argued that real decisions are made
    incrementally — successive limited comparisons, "muddling through" — because
    comprehensive analysis is impossible, values conflict, and politics negotiates; and
    Jane Jacobs (1961) showed with close street observation that the city is an organic
    system whose ordered vitality (mixed uses, short blocks, old buildings, dense
    sidewalk life) is exactly what functionalist planning deleted. The rational model
    survived both critiques, but demoted: one instrument in the kit, valid when goals
    are agreed and effects measurable. A metro-line appraisal shows the model working as
    designed: goals named (ridership, emissions, access to jobs), alternatives generated
    (bus rapid transit, light rail, heavy rail, no-build), each evaluated over decades of
    costs and benefits in a single comparable table, the discount rate argued openly, the
    choice documented. Where goals are genuinely shared, this machinery disciplines power
    rather than serving it — which is precisely why its failures elsewhere teach so
    much.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <em>communicative turn</em> rebuilt the field around the insight that planning
    is, at bottom, structured argument about a shared future. Drawing on Habermas's
    theory of communicative action, theorists such as Patsy Healey and John Forester
    re-described the planner's core skill as convening: bringing stakeholders to the
    table, brokering information so that all parties can actually understand it,
    listening for the power dynamics that silence some voices, and legitimating
    decisions through genuine dialogue rather than expert decree. Arnstein's ladder
    (1969) remains the field's sharpest auditing tool for this practice: participation
    runs from manipulation and therapy at the bottom, through informing and consultation
    (rungs where citizens hear and are heard but do not decide), to partnership,
    delegated power and citizen control at the top — and too much real-world
    "engagement" lives on the lower rungs while wearing the vocabulary of the upper.
    The practice methods built on this theory are now standard kit: design charrettes
    that run for days instead of one hostile evening, citizens' assemblies and
    deliberative polls that pay participants to reason together, participatory
    budgeting (pioneered in Porto Alegre in 1989, now global) that hands real money to
    real decisions, and third-party facilitation when the room's own power dynamics
    would devour the conversation. Each tool is an attempt to move a specific step up
    Arnstein's ladder — and each fails exactly as often as it mistakes the rung for
    the relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <em>just city</em> tradition asks the normative question both predecessors
    skirted: who gets what from the city, and who decides? David Harvey's <em>Social
    Justice and the City</em> (1973) reframed planning as distribution under capital —
    land, rent and whose profit the plan serves — and Henri Lefebvre's "right to the
    city" gave the movement its slogan. Susan Fainstein's <em>The Just City</em> (2010),
    testing New York, London and Amsterdam, settled on three evaluative criteria —
    <strong>equity</strong> (distribution of benefit), <strong>diversity</strong> (who
    can live where) and <strong>democracy</strong> (who decides) — and, crucially,
    acknowledged that the three trade off: a deeply democratic process can still produce
    exclusionary outcomes, and an equitable outcome can arrive top-down. Planning theory
    after 2000 is largely the management of that triangle.
  </p>

  <h2>Planning ethics</h2>
  <p>
    Planning ethics is unusually concrete, because planning decisions redistribute value
    by the million: a single rezoning can transfer enormous wealth between owners, and a
    highway alignment decides which neighbourhood absorbs the noise. The professional
    codes (AICP in the US, the RTPI's in the UK) converge on durable principles: serve
    the public interest while acknowledging it is plural and contested; give affected
    people a genuine voice; protect the interests of those least able to defend them;
    disclose conflicts of interest; and be truthful with data — a planner's forecasts
    are advocacy with decimal points, and the profession's history of watch-dial
    projections justifying highways is the cautionary tale.
  </p>
  <p>
    The recurring dilemma runs straight through the theory chapters. Imagine a
    well-attended consultation where homeowners oppose a modest apartment building —
    authentic voices, authentically heard — while the people who would live in it, and
    who are excluded by the very scarcity the objectors defend, are absent by
    definition. Pure communicative ethics sides with the meeting; pure just-city ethics
    sides with the absent; the rational model points at housing-supply data. Real
    planners resolve this daily with documented judgement — weighing whom the decision
    actually binds, and owning the values doing the weighing — which is why the codes
    demand honesty about values rather than pretending the values are not there.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper layers complete the theorist's toolkit. The <em>implementation gap</em>:
    plans are promises made in one political moment and kept (or not) across many;
    scholars of the gap study why adopted plans so often die in the budget process, and
    treat planners as street-level bureaucrats whose everyday discretion, not the plan
    document, is where policy actually happens. <em>Informality</em>: most of the
    world's urban growth happens outside formal planning — informal settlements house
    roughly a billion people — and planning theory built for surveyed, zoned cities
    struggles with (and increasingly learns from) incremental, self-built urbanism.
    <em>Climate and technology</em>: adaptation planning introduces decisions under deep
    uncertainty (which coasts to defend, which to retreat from — with distributional
    questions the just-city lens was built for), while the "smart city" promises of
    data-driven management replay the rationalist debate with sensors: optimise for
    whom, and who audits the algorithm? The century's arc — vision, technique,
    critique, conversation, justice — looks likely to repeat itself once more, at
    machine speed.
  </p>

